## 6. Form performance

### 6.1 Questions not answered on completed forms

Share of completed cases carrying each nature where the specific-problem question is blank: envenomation 2.6 per cent (5 of 195), environmental 1.8 per cent (1 of 57), injury 0.3 per cent (3 of 984), medical 0 per cent (0 of 137), suspected drowning 0 per cent (0 of 12), mental health 0 per cent (0 of 4). 2.4 per cent of injury cases did not record a body location; 1.7 per cent of completed cases did not record a nature, 2.2 per cent no treatment, 2.1 per cent no discharge pathway. No malformed duplicate multiselect values exist in the core fields of the current export.

### 6.2 Option lists that need work

Free-text "Other" overflow by branch: environmental 30.4 per cent (17 of 56), envenomation 13.6 per cent (26 of 191), medical 10.2 per cent (14 of 137), nature 1.6 per cent, injury 1.1 per cent. The environmental free text is dominated by cold and heat exposure variants and dizziness; the envenomation free text is mostly unidentified stings and insect stings. The change register already includes option-list and field changes (SpO2 field, barriers optional, renames).

## 7. Outliers explained

Every exception quoted above is accounted for here from the structured fields of the completed records concerned.

- **2.6 per cent of envenomation cases did not record the specific problem.** Five completed forms where the member passed the step without selecting an option; all five received sting-typical treatment (ice or hot water), and two of the five are classified major with no referral and no ambulance. The workflow allows the question to be skipped on completion; making it mandatory would close the gap.
- **0.3 per cent of injury cases and 1.8 per cent of environmental cases did not record the specific problem.** Three completed injury forms and one completed environmental form (treatment "Other") skipped the step; none is classified major.
- **Eleven of twelve suspected drownings are major.** The twelfth is a member of the public aged 12, recorded as a non-fatal suspected drowning, conscious at discharge, treatment "Other", no referral, no ambulance, no incident number and no narrative. The structured record describes a low-acuity episode the crew did not escalate; the absence of any narrative or incident link for a suspected drowning is itself a consistency point for the action-form work.
- **Three of four mental health cases are major.** The fourth is a panic attack in a 27-year-old, no treatment required, no referral; classified minor consistently with those fields.
- **Five resuscitation-nature cases, seven resuscitation records.** Two cardiac arrests were recorded under the medical nature with the resuscitation set completed. The question-set count (seven) is the reliable one.
- **Envenomation 3.6 per cent major (seven cases).** Three stingray injuries (two to hospital with an adult, one by ambulance), one combined medical and envenomation case transported by ambulance, one jellyfish sting with no referral, and the two majors with no specific problem and no referral above. Stingray injuries drive the severity; the last three are classification inconsistencies rather than clinical escalations.
- **Thirty-eight completed cases documented a day or more after treatment.** Thirty-one injuries; ten major; eighteen from club-event cohorts; two volunteer clubs account for sixteen of the thirty-eight. The pattern is retrospective entry after club events at a small number of units, not a system-wide lag.
- **Three cases record treatment after the form was created** (44 minutes, 10 hours and 9 days later). Treatment-time entry errors; excluded from timeliness figures.
- **Twenty-four completed forms (1.7 per cent) have no nature.** All have a free-text reason and a treatment, and all workflow steps are marked complete. The nature question can be passed without an answer; it should be mandatory because every downstream question set keys off it.
- **232 completed cases classified major, 253 incident-linked.** The flags are set independently: 5 majors have no incident number and 26 incident-linked cases are not major, 31 completed records (2.2 per cent) where classification and link disagree. A further 17 open forms are flagged major with no link (section 8).
- **Fourteen methoxyflurane records, eight pain trajectories.** Six have no starting score. Two of the eight start at 1 of 10; the remaining six start at 8 to 10 and fall 0 to 6 points by four minutes.

